  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>Solve each equation. Be prepared to explain or show your reasoning.</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(x^2=121\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the solution(s) to the equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(x=11\) and \(x=-11\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(x^2−31=5\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the solution(s) to the equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(x=6\) and \(x=-6\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((x−4)(x−4)=0\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the solution(s) to the equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(x=4\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((x+3)(x−1)=5\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the solution(s) to the equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(x=-4\) and \(x=2\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((x+1)^2=-4\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the solution(s) to the equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>No solution</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((x−4)(x−1)=990\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the solution(s) to the equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(x=34\) and \(x=-29\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<title>Video: Choosing an Effective Strategy to Solve Quadratic Equations</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about choosing an effective strategy to solve quadratic equations.</para>
<media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text"><iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/84d199f48e88269b0d50c3dc76afa178bc452da3"/></media></section>

<note class="self-check" id="sect-00001"><para id="para-00001">Solve the equation using any method covered in the lesson.</para>
  <para id="para-00002">\((x−6)(x+5)=-30.25\)</para></note>

  <section>
    <title>Choosing an Effective Strategy to Solve Quadratic Equations</title>
    <para>You have learned various strategies to solve quadratic equations in different forms. If possible, try to use reasoning before graphing an equation.</para>
    <para>Let's look at some examples.</para>
    <para>
     <emphasis>Using Reasoning and the Square Root Property</emphasis>
   </para>
    <para>Equations such as these can be solved by reasoning and using the <term data-schema-version="“1.0”" class="glossary-tooltip">square root property</term>.</para>
    <para>Example 1</para>
    <para> \(x^2=81\) 
\(x=9\) and \(x=-9\)</para>
    <para>Example 2</para>
    <para> \(x^2+7=56\)
\(x^2=49\)
\(x=7\) and \(x=-7\)</para>
    <para>Example 3</para>
    <para> \((x+1)^2=9\)
\(x=2\) and \(x=-4\)</para>
    <para>Each equation above is different in some way, but they all can be solved using the same process.</para>
    <para>
     <emphasis>Using Reasoning and the Zero Product Property</emphasis>
   </para>
    <para>Equations such as these can be solved by reasoning and using the <term data-schema-version="“1.0”" class="glossary-tooltip">zero product property</term>.</para>
    <para>Example 1</para>
    <para> \((x−4)(x+5)=0\)
\(x=4\) and \(x=-5\)</para>
    <para>Example 2</para>
    <para> \((x+3)(2x+7)=0\)
\(x=-3\) and \(x=-\frac{7}{2}\)</para>
    <para>
     <emphasis>Using Graphing to Solve</emphasis>
   </para>
    <para>Some equations might require graphing to be solved. One method is to rearrange the equation such that it is in the form of <emphasis>expression</emphasis> \(= 0\). Then graph \(y\) =<emphasis>expression</emphasis>, and find the \(x\)-intercepts to identify the solutions.</para>
    <para>Given: \((x+2)(x−1)=4\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Rewrite the equation to set it equal to 0.</para>
    <para> \((x+2)(x−1)−4=0\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Graph the equation with \(y\) = the expression that equals zero.</para>
    <para> Graph \(y=(x+2)(x−1)−4\).</para>
    <para>
      <figure>
        <media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UP AND PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS (NEGATIVE 3, 0) AND (2, 0).">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_07_08_01.jpg"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
   </para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Identify the \(x\)-intercepts from the graph.</para>
    <para> This graph has two \(x\)-intercepts at \((-3,0)\) and \((2,0)\).</para>
    <para>The original equation \((x+2)(x−1)=4\) has two solutions at \(x=-3\) and \(x=2\).</para>
    <note class="try look-at-me"/>
<title>Try It: Choosing an Effective Strategy to Solve Quadratic Equations</title>
    <para>Solve each equation using any method.</para>
    <example>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\((x+1)^2+6=22\)</para>
          <para>Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Here is how to choose an effective strategy and use it to solve quadratic equations: </para>
          <para>\((x+1)^2+6=22\) 
  \((x+1)^2=16\)
  \(x=-5\) and \(x=3\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </example>
    <example>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\((x+3)(x−8)=0\)</para>
          <para>Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Here is how to choose an effective strategy and use it to solve quadratic equations: </para>
          <para>\((x+3)(x−8)=0\) 
  \(x=-3\) and \(x=8\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </example>
  </section>
